hey it's so wonderful to see you again  yes  i i must confess that until earlier  today i didn't realize how historic your  performance tonight  was going to be i didn't need to be  honest i'm still i can't believe that  when someone said it to me i'm like what  wow so talk a little bit  or a lot about that and what it means to  you  and then what it means for us as a  community  well that whole phrase you see it you  can be it  really rings true and the only reason  why  i felt that i could pursue country music  was because i saw another black woman  singing country music named reecy palmer  and if it wasn't for her i wouldn't be  here so so me standing on that stage  representing just women of color period  it shows them that they have the  opportunity to and that they can sing  whatever kind of music they want to sing  it doesn't just have to be just r b if  they want to sing country music  they can and they will be accepted by  this beautiful community  so you were introduced by tim mcgraw and  then accompanied by keith urban you know  it doesn't get much bigger than that  talk about what it means to you to be  embraced by the country community  tonight  you know i've been pursuing music for a  very long time  and just to feel embraced by the country  music community i've always  known that they've been so loving and to  experience  it for the first time like that like it  has just been  overwhelming i'm pregnant and hormonal  so i've been trying not to cry this  whole time  but it is just it really does mean so  much to me  to be embraced by this community i know  we've been talking so much all year long  about diversity in country music and a  lack of representation  and i know you've always said that you  just really sing your truth so  what was going through your mind tonight  while you were singing your truth on  stage well first of all i have  three amazing canadian songwriters that  wrote this song with me  victoria banks emily karen kasowski who  wrote and also produced it  and that first of all means so much to  me and  and just just stand here and sing such a  polarizing song  on the acm awards about protecting our  future  protecting our daughters protecting  women because we're so  important and we matter and it's just  been such an honor to be able to stand  here  and and and be that to people i just  wanted to ask you  obviously for so many reasons 2020 has  been  such a monumental year and one thing  that i am hopeful about and that i  am excited i don't know if that's the  right word is that  it seems like we're finally going beyond  just talking about change needs to  happen  so do you feel that do you feel like  this is an actual shift and  just share your thoughts on the fact  that it seems like we may actually be  turning at least a little bit of a point  absolutely  i am a witness to it i have seen it i'm  a part of the acm diversity task force  and the acms are relentlessly committed  to diversifying the ac awards there's a  black writer  on the show and they've really taken a  lot of our recommendations on how to  make this world so inclusive because it  actually is  all people have to do is see themselves  and feel encouraged to be a part of this  community  and they can and the acms really has  shown that tonight  and i'm just like you i feel really  really hopeful like it has just been  as hard of a year as it's been as crazy  as the year it's been  it really has been beautiful to see  people come together and put all of our  differences aside and make the world  better
 

